# A seven-segment digit counting through 0-F — the first example that drives
# a display rather than a lamp.
#
# This is 02_7_segment from treideme/stc89c52-demos (Apache-2.0) said in the
# dialect. It is here because that corpus measured it as unreachable: it
# needed whole-port I/O and an indexed lookup table, and nothing else. Both
# now exist, so this example is the evidence rather than the claim.
# See docs/DIALECT-COVERAGE.md.
#
#   P0.0-P0.6  segments a-g, P0.7 the decimal point
#   common cathode, so a 1 lights a segment
#
# Wire each segment through its own resistor. A common-anode display wants
# `PORT segments = P0 OUTPUT ACTIVE LOW` instead, and nothing else changes --
# which is the point of putting polarity in the declaration.
#
# Two things worth noticing about what this compiles to:
#
# The font lands in code space as `const __code`, not in the 256 bytes of RAM.
# Flash is the abundant resource on this part.
#
# `font[digit]` with a computed index is clamped, not trusted. Reading past a
# table means reading a random byte of flash and displaying it, which looks
# like data rather than like a fault. A constant index is checked when it
# compiles and costs nothing.

DEVICE STC12C5A60S2:
  CLOCK 11059200

  #             dGFEDCBA
  TABLE font = 0b00111111, 0b00000110, 0b01011011, 0b01001111, 0b01100110, 0b01101101, 0b01111101, 0b00000111, 0b01111111, 0b01101111, 0b01110111, 0b01111100, 0b00111001, 0b01011110, 0b01111001, 0b01110001

  PORT segments = P0 OUTPUT              # PDIP-40 pins 39 down to 32

  WHEN started:
    set digit to 0
    FOREVER:
      set segments to font[digit]
      wait 500 ms
      change digit by 1
      IF digit > 15 THEN:
        set digit to 0
